# Issue #927: A list element moved out by index (`out.push(xs[i])`, String) reaches rustc instead of a refusal naming the copy

[Open and answer this issue on GitHub](https://github.com/Nikaia-Foundation/Nikaia/issues/927).

`ready` · opened 2026-10-28 · updated 2026-10-31 · 1 comment

**Probe**
```nika
fn lines() -> Vec[String] {
    return ["a", "b"]
}

fn main() {
    let rest = lines()
    let mut out: Vec[String] = []
    for i in 0..<rest.len() {
        out.push(rest[i])
    }
    println(out.join(","))
}
```
The lowering is `out.push(*nikaia_std::index::get(&rest, i as usize))`, and rustc answers `Cannot move out of a shared reference`.

The language never copies unseen (Part I, `with`: "never copied unseen … the refusal names the copy to write"), so `rest[i].clone()` is the right program. But the checker should say so itself, as an `NK` refusal with the help `rest[i].clone()`, and the error should not be rustc's. This was met in the converter (`tr_stmt.nika`) and corrected there with `.clone()`.

**To build**
1. In the checker, refuse an element of a list or map, read by index, of a type that is neither `Copy` nor a view, when it is handed somewhere that keeps it (a call argument by value, `let`, `return`). The help writes `.clone()`. This needs a new `NK` code from the supervisor's block.
2. Add a `tests/compiler/tests/*.nika` + `.stderr`.

