# Issue #929: A struct that holds a text read at run time and hands out views of it: the checker accepts, rustc refuses; `ref String` field refuses the text (blocks a pull parser, #928)

[Open and answer this issue on GitHub](https://github.com/Nikaia-Foundation/Nikaia/issues/929).

`wip` · opened 2026-10-28 · updated 2026-10-29 · 4 comments

## Found while

Checking what the `yaml` package (#928) needs from the language: a pull / event parser is a struct that holds the input and a position, and whose `next` returns views into the input. With `target/fast/nikaia` on `develop` (2026-10-28):

### Probe 1: field `input: String`, the text read at run time

```nika
use std::fs

enum Value {
    Str(String),
    Seq(Vec[Value]),
}

struct Cursor {
    input: String,
    pos: i64,
}

fn next_item(mut c: Cursor) -> String? {
    let rest = c.input.lines().skip(c.pos).collect()
    if rest.len() == 0 {
        return null
    }
    c.pos += 1
    return rest[0].trim()
}

fn parse(input: String) -> Value {
    let mut items: Vec[Value] = []
    for l in input.lines() {
        items.push(Value::Str(l.trim()))
    }
    return Value::Seq(items)
}

fn main() throws {
    let text = fs::read_to_string("in.yaml", fs::Root::Anywhere)
    let v = parse(text)
    let mut c = Cursor { input: text, pos: 0 }
    while true {
        let s = next_item(c)
        if s == null { break }
        println(f"pull: {s}")
    }
    match v {
        Value::Seq(items) => {
            for it in items {
                match it {
                    Value::Str(s) => println(s),
                    else => println("?"),
                }
            }
        },
        else => println("?"),
    }
}
```

