# Issue #985: Two overlap branches that update one SharedMut are accepted; Part II 12.2 says two writing doors on one lock collide (NK2104)

[Open and answer this issue on GitHub](https://github.com/Nikaia-Foundation/Nikaia/issues/985).

`ready` · opened 2026-10-30 · updated 2026-10-30 · 0 comments

Found while preparing ADR-async-1 (#983), checked on develop 4f0372c with `target/fast/nikaia check`.

**Probe** (`a.nika`):

    fn main() {
        let seen = SharedMut(0)
        let p = overlap {
            seen.update fn(mut v) { v += 1 }
            seen.update fn(mut v) { v += 2 }
        }
        println(f"{seen.get()}")
    }

`nikaia check` prints `Checked a.nika`. Expected: `NK2104` at the second branch, naming the lock: Part II 12.2 says *`set` and `update` are writes ... Any two of the writing forms are ordered by the rule that orders two `println`s*, and two `println` branches are refused today (`Both touch stdout, and one writes to it`). The walk does not account a lock reached through a local binding, so it falls under C.4 (not accounted, not refused), though the binding is a plain local the walk can see.

The same shape is in `tests/language/src/task_scope.nika` tests 1 and 3 (two `seen.update` tasks); under `async` (ADR-async-1 D8) they would be refused once this is fixed, and the migration rewrites them with the group's value.

## To build
1. In the touch walk (`crates/nikaia-std/src/tools/touch.nika`, `check_walks.nika`), a door (`get`/`access` read, `set`/`update` write) on a `SharedMut` or `Locked` bound to a local or a parameter is a touch of that lock, named by the binding.
2. Two branches writing one such lock: `NK2104`, *both reach the lock `seen`, and one writes it*; two `get`s: accepted.
3. Tests: `tests/compiler/tests/overlap_two_updates_one_lock.nika` (`.stderr` with `NK2104`), `overlap_two_gets_one_lock.nika` (`.stdout`), `overlap_updates_two_locks.nika` (accepted).


